# Canonical author identity for this repository.
#
# Seven commits were authored with a local machine identity instead of the
# GitHub noreply address used by the other 110. This file maps them onto the
# canonical identity so `git log`, `git shortlog`, `git blame` and `git log
# --format=%aN/%aE` all show one contributor instead of three.
#
# Format: Canonical Name <canonical-email> <email-as-recorded-in-the-commit>
# Email matching is case-insensitive, so the single line below also covers the
# capitalised variant of the same address that two of the commits carry.
#
# What this does NOT do, stated plainly:
#
#   1. It does not change stored history. The commit objects still carry the
#      original address. `git cat-file -p <sha>` and `git log --format=%ae`
#      (lower-case %ae bypasses mailmap) both still return it.
#   2. It does not change what GitHub serves. GitHub does not apply .mailmap
#      to its commit views, blame, or contributor graph, and the REST commit
#      API returns the raw recorded author address regardless of this file.
#
# So this is a canonicalisation for local git tooling, not a scrub. Removing
# the address from stored history would require rewriting commits, which is
# out of scope here.

